\section{Introduction}

This study investigates the event horizons of a new class of asymptotically flat black holes that possess hair composed of a complex scalar field within the Einstein-gravity framework coupled to a complex scalar field interacting minimally with both Einstein-Maxwell gravity and a curvature-squared Riemann term. The inclusion of the Riemann term in our gravity theory permits horizon-penetrating metric ans\"{a}tze, enabling us to explicitly analyze the event horizons of these black holes. This capability allows us to derive analytical expressions for various horizon characteristics without relying on numerical methods.

These black holes emerge from an underlying family of asymptotically anti-de Sitter (AdS) black holes, which serve as gravito-condensates for a specific conformal field theory arising from the Kerr-Sen AdS black hole's near-horizon limit \cite{KerrSenAdSlim, KSS}. Consequently, the non-trivial scalar profiles of this new black hole family, outside the event horizon, correspond to the condensed scalar modes of the Higgs sector in the AdS black hole limit. Thus, within this family, the hairy black hole solution functions as a classical analogue to the Kerr-Sen AdS \cite{KSS} holographic superconductor.

